+# HyperFlux V2 receiver management (experimental Nobara backport)
+
+Observed on a physical 1532:00cf HyperFlux V2 Hard Edition with a paired Naga
+V2 Pro, using Synapse 4.0.842 on Windows 11 on 2026-09-26. Passive tracing of
+Synapse's HID API calls and completed feature replies, not a USB bus capture.
+The tracer filters handles by VID/PID and never modifies reports. The report
+ID zero prefix in Windows buffers is not part of the 90-byte Razer packet.
+
+Verified exchanges, all with matching transaction IDs and valid XOR checksums:
+
+| Purpose | Transaction | Class/command | Size | Request arguments | Reply |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| Disable seamless auto-pair | f1 | 00/55 | 1 | 00 | success, 00 |
+| Enable seamless auto-pair | f2 | 00/55 | 1 | 01 | success, 01 |
+| Receiver inventory | f3–f9 | 00/bf | 80 | all zero | success, 02 01 00 a8 00 ff ff, then zero |
+| Enable health optimizer, 50% | 00 | 07/12 | 1 | b2 | busy, then **not supported (05)** |
+| Enable health optimizer, 80% | 00 | 07/12 | 1 | d0 | busy, then **not supported (05)** |
+| Disable health optimizer, 50% | 00 | 07/12 | 1 | 32 | busy, then **not supported (05)** |
+
+The successful auto-pair writes were observed at 13:08:21.835Z and
+13:08:22.839Z (replies 13:08:21.926Z / 13:08:22.933Z). Inventory was read when
+opening the device and pairing pages. No keyboard was listed on these pages.
+This is not a verification of keyboard pairing, unpairing, or all inventory
+flags. Unknown layouts/slots remain unknown; they are not called unpaired.
+
+## Kernel/userspace contract
+
+Only interface 4 exposes `hyperflux_inventory` (80 argument bytes as hex),
+`hyperflux_auto_pair` (write-only boolean), and the typed pairing attributes
+described below. This is independent of whether a
+supported mouse was present when the driver bound. The receiver uses interface
+0, report zero, transaction high nibble f; the mouse's existing route is 1f
+and the keyboard's route is 9f. All paths use the **same per-interface-4 mutex**, including SET and all response
+reads. Other HID interfaces continue to pass input through unchanged.
+
+The exchange sends SET once, bounds response reads, checks framing, checksum,
+identity and status, and propagates errors. Auto-pair also verifies the echoed
+value. There is no raw-report write API or guessed pair/unpair command.
+Management attribute creation failure must not prevent HID input binding.
+
+`razer.device.hyperflux.getReceiverInfo` returns JSON with explicit provenance:
+`live-receiver-report`, `driver-binding-snapshot` (older kernel), or `unavailable`.
+A failed live query never falls back to the snapshot. `setAutoPair(b)` checks its
+kernel attribute at call time; controls appear only when it is present. Neither device initialization nor
+opening the UI restores/persists settings or sends writes.
+
+No auto-pair getter has been verified. `auto_pair` therefore remains null, and
+`auto_pair_last_ack` is only the last acknowledged command in this daemon
+session, **not a current-state reading**. The UI uses explicit Enable/Disable
+buttons rather than a switch with invented initial state.
+
+Charging policy is deliberately not exposed: Synapse changed its UI even when
+the firmware returned NOT_SUPPORTED. Firmware updating is outside these tests.
+At the user's request, release 8 generalizes the captured mouse/keyboard pairing
+sequences to all compatible PIDs reported by those receiver slots. Untested
+models are enabled with a notice rather than disabled. Subsequent captures below
+also verify Naga V3 pairing/unpairing.
+Keyboard lighting and other controls are provided by a separate logical child;
+arbitrary key remapping and Synapse onboard profiles are not implemented.
+Existing pairings are never changed by Linux discovery.
+
+Baseline UI settings were restored: auto-pair on; optimizer off; inactive limit
+50%. This does not turn the rejected charging replies into successful writes.
+
+## Subsequent USB bus evidence: manual pairing
+
+On the same physical Windows host, pad-only USBPcap captures (bus2/address1)
+established a complete off-mat Naga V2 cycle and a BlackWidow V4 Tenkeyless
+HyperSpeed cycle. The keyboard needed a physical power-cycle/wake before its
+successful re-pair. These captures do not qualify Naga V3 manual pairing,
+other keyboard models, or a software-only recovery guarantee.
+
+All listed feature commands use wIndex0, feature report0, length90; SET once,
+then read a matching, checksum-valid successful reply. The transaction values
+are observations, not permission to substitute arbitrary routing values.
+
+| Operation | Transaction | Class/command | Size | Arguments |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| Keyboard discovery | 80 | 00/46 | 1 | 01 |
+| Mouse discovery | 00 | 00/46 | 1 | 01 |
+| Pair BlackWidow V4 TKL HyperSpeed | 80 | 00/41 | 3 | 01 02 d5 |
+| Unpair that keyboard | 00 | 00/42 | 2 | 02 d5 |
+| Pair Naga V2 Pro | 00 | 00/41 | 3 | 01 00 a8 |
+| Unpair Naga V2 Pro (mat-page control) | 80 | 00/42 | 2 | 00 a8 |
+
+Discovery completes asynchronously after approximately 10 seconds on interrupt
+endpoint82. Keyboard notifications use report09, mouse report05. All observed
+notifications here are 16 bytes. Keyboard scan start is `09 37 01`, followed by
+zero padding. Its candidate response is `09 37 02 01 02 d5 01`, then nine zeros;
+the meaning of the extra01 after PID is unverified. Mouse candidate response
+is `05 37 02 01 00 a8` followed by ten zeros. An empty keyboard scan produces
+`09 37 02` followed by thirteen zeros. Do not invent multi-candidate layouts.
+
+Keyboard pair start/completion are `09 36 01` / `09 36 02`, each zero-padded;
+mouse equivalents use05. A successful feature ACK is **not** completed pairing.
+One keyboard re-pair ACKed successfully but never completed and never restored
+inventory. A later scan coincided with `09 36 03`; its precise failure/cancel
+semantics and any safe cancellation command remain unverified.
+
+Inventory 00/bf starts with02 and two flag/PID slots: mouse at bytes1–3,
+keyboard at4–6, remainder zero in these traces. Both paired reads
+`02 01 00 a8 01 02 d5`; keyboard unpair gives `02 01 00 a8 00 ff ff` and does not
+clear the mouse PID. During discovery either flag can become00 while its PID
+remains present. A flag alone must not be treated as persistent pairing status.
+
+Original capture SHA-256 values (private evidence files; not shipped in RPMs):
+
+- Naga V2 cycle002: `c5cfae25ad05f7d04ebc4c581c54e2134e249a7fbbe03bde81d8f4016c791add`
+- Keyboard cycle002, successful first pair/unpair and incomplete re-pair:
+  `433ebb62d3319a9d42eeeda1461406b49f6aea368838183a89e354be02ca9f03`
+- Keyboard cycle003, successful re-pair after wake:
+  `d4320486fcfe7228c06441de9180f026d78c782a746d4b7548e9a325622f1960`
+
+The initial offline decoder/evidence suite had 25 passing tests, including the incomplete
+attempt, separate completion/inventory checks, and keyboard feature traffic.
+Keyboard feature controls and release9 dynamic membership handling are described below.
+Charging policy still requires separate validation; later captures ACKed a disabled
+optimizer value, which does not establish enabled limits or enforcement.
+Linux end-to-end pairing still requires user testing.
+
+## Typed pairing implementation (RPM release 8)
+
+- `hyperflux_scan`: write `mouse` or `keyboard`. One captured scan command,
+  matching ACK, then at most 15 seconds waiting for validated notifications.
+  The read-only `hyperflux_pairing_status` exposes the reported candidate PID, its mouse/keyboard type, and
+  single-use random token, valid for 60 seconds. No implicit pair command.
+- `hyperflux_pair`: write `<16-hex-token> <4-hex-PID>`. Reject stale candidates,
+  invalid/reserved PIDs, and any occupied destination slot. The notification
+  report (05 mouse / 09 keyboard), not a model whitelist or PID range, selects
+  the slot. Send one pair
+  command; require ACK, bounded notification completion (60 seconds), and fresh
+  matching inventory while preserving the other slot's PID.
+- `hyperflux_unpair`: write the actual paired PID. Re-read inventory and require
+  exactly one matching slot before
+  sending one PID-specific command. Require ACK and cleared destination slot,
+  with the other PID preserved. Seamless auto-pair may subsequently reconnect
+  an on-mat mouse, so remove it from the charging surface first.
+- Status exposes protocol_version=2, ready/busy/blocked/error/candidate/
+  candidate_type/token, never raw HID data. Candidate type is empty when the
+  token is absent/expired. Generic PID syntax is validated by kernel, daemon,
+  and client; only receiver discovery can authorize a new pair, and only live
+  inventory can authorize an unpair. Capture coverage is advisory metadata.
+  Any uncertain sent operation invalidates discovery and blocks subsequent
+  pairing until physical receiver reconnect. There is no guessed cancellation
+  or write retry. Reconnecting invalidates all tokens and pending operations.
+
+A refcounted USB-device-scoped context bridges interface 1 notifications to the
+interface 4 command endpoint. Interface 1 holds a balanced `hid_hw_open` reference
+so notifications also work without another input client. Every raw input report
+is still returned untouched to HID. The IRQ callback performs no USB I/O, sleep,
+input remapping, or event consumption. Failed management setup does not fail
+input binding. Disconnect wakes pending operations before releasing the context.
+
+Long pairing operations hold the same feature mutex as mouse controls. Other
+HyperFlux control requests return EBUSY rather than blocking the main loop;
+ordinary mouse/keyboard input uses independent interrupt endpoints. Mouse probe
+and transaction routing now use verified 00/bf inventory (including its keyboard
+slot), replacing the older mouse-only 512-byte layout check.
+
+DBus `startScan(s)`, `pairDevice(ss)`, and `unpairDevice(s)` return a daemon job
+ID promptly. A single daemon worker performs the bounded sysfs operation, with
+no retries or path rediscovery. `getPairingStatus()` returns JSON job state and
+kernel status. Polychromatic polls status, requires explicit pair/unpair
+confirmation (default No), and refreshes inventory after completion. Closing
+the page is not cancellation. No writes occur on opening the page or daemon
+startup. Do not run another raw-HID configuration client concurrently.
+
+## Keyboard feature route and logical child
+
+The same original keyboard cycle002 PCAP contains feature traffic when Synapse
+opens the keyboard Customize page. Matching SET/GET, valid CRC, interface0 and
+USB success are checked by the offline fixtures. Examples (frame numbers):
+
+- 4617/4619: tx81, 00:87 firmware reply 01040200.
+- 4705/4707: tx86, 07:84 charging reply 0000.
+- 4713/4715: tx87, 07:80 battery reply 0099 (60%).
+- 5051/5053: tx81, 0f:84 brightness query 010500, reply 01054c.
+- 4863/4865: tx9b, 00:c0 polling query 0100, reply 0108 (1000 Hz).
+- 4811/4813: tx94, 0f:04 brightness write 01004c, matching SUCCESS.
+- 6807/6809: tx8a, 0f:02 custom effect 000008000000, matching SUCCESS.
+
+Keyboard commands have the high routing bit set (80..9f), unlike the mouse.
+Upstream wireless keyboard handlers already use 9f. The new driver reuses the
+existing extended keyboard command builders and that route, serialized with
+the mouse/receiver commands. Specific effect variants and other keyboard models
+are implementation generalizations, not additional hardware capture coverage.
+
+Release9 observes the keyboard slot every three seconds in a read-only kernel
+worker, even when auto-pairing is disabled. While a keyboard occupies it,
+interface4 exposes a `hyperflux_keyboard_<pid>/` sysfs group (older modules
+use `hyperflux_keyboard/`). Each feature transaction first verifies that
+the slot still contains that group's bound PID; a replacement or empty slot
+fails instead of redirecting cached controls. Attributes include brightness,
+battery/charging, firmware, game-mode LED, basic/extended lighting effects,
+bounded per-key rows for known matrix geometries, and the upstream 125–1000 Hz
+polling interface for BlackWidow V4 Tenkeyless HyperSpeed. No input translation,
+driver-mode writes, raw-report interface or arbitrary remapping is introduced.
+
+The daemon registers a separate `<keyboard name> (HyperFlux V2)` child with
+receiver-scoped identity and paths. Wireless model metadata supplies matrix
+dimensions and artwork for BlackWidow V3 Pro, V3 Mini, V4 Mini, V4 Tenkeyless,
+DeathStalker V2 Pro and V2 Pro TKL. Unknown models use a PID-labelled entry and
+common controls, with no invented matrix layout. Firmware errors propagate.
+Different models use distinct DBus classes so their capabilities cannot leak
+across pads. USB removal removes the child; discovering it does not restore
+brightness/effects, set a polling rate, or enter proprietary driver mode.
+
+On a keyboard membership change, the old group is removed and active sysfs
+callbacks drain before changing its identity. A PID-specific new path prevents
+stale daemon objects controlling a different keyboard. KOBJ_CHANGE triggers a
+permissions-only udev helper (no input rebind) and daemon child reconciliation,
+which emits existing device-added/removed signals to Polychromatic. The daemon
+also discovers these groups on startup; it does not run an auto-pair loop.
+At release 9 the mouse child still had a probe-time binding. Release 13 below
+replaces it with dynamic PID-specific controls, removing the reconnect requirement.
+Linux end-to-end keyboard controls and pairing remain for user testing;
+successful compilation/mock tests are not hardware validation.
+
+## Driver-managed automatic pairing (RPM release 9)
+
+The awake, puck-removed/off-mat Windows V3 test established host-driven pairing
+without clicking Pair. Capture `hyperflux-puck-removed-002.pcap`, SHA256
+`9c2149e1c9cb70c6a19d3f9f5988dbaee316f8efcdab6da263beb677691d760a`,
+contains V3 discovery5831, automatic host00/41 tx00 `0100e8`5839/5847,
+separate053602completion5904, and confirmed inventory5909 onward. User confirmed
+pairing. The prior empty scan does not prove a puck requirement or establish
+sleep as its sole cause. Earlier successful V3 unpair/re-pair capture SHA256:
+`fad530a8300c7bebc608c0e6e2ee07bdd22c31df48e551b1f6bc83bf86e5bdc7`.
+
+`hyperflux_driver_auto_pair` accepts mask0..3: bit0 mouse, bit1 keyboard. Both
+default off per USB connection. `setDriverAutoPair(bb)` and the Python/GUI
+controls only write this policy. It survives daemon/GUI exit but not USB
+disconnect/module reload; no startup persistence is applied. This is Linux
+driver behavior, not a claimed firmware/puck detection feature. The existing
+00/55 `hyperflux_auto_pair` firmware flag remains separate, with ACK-only state.
+
+A freezable delayed kernel work item serializes fresh inventory, discovery and
+pairing through the existing interface4 mutex. It chooses only enabled EMPTY
+slots, fairly alternates when both are eligible, and pauses15s after each cycle.
+Presence flags do not make a stored PID empty. Discovery must return one
+validated candidate of the requested slot; it uses a fresh single-use token,
+rechecks destination inventory before pairing, and requires ACK, completion
+and target/other-slot verification. No unpair/replacement is automatic. A failed
+or ambiguous operation disables both auto slots; uncertain sent operations
+retain the existing reconnect-required block. Empty successful scans may repeat.
+
+Protocol3 `hyperflux_pairing_status` includes `driver_auto_pair` with actual
+kernel mouse/keyboard enables, phase/target, errno and successful-pair revision.
+It additionally reports completed discovery `scans`, dispatched `pair_attempts`,
+`empty_slots` (mouse bit0, keyboard bit1 at the last auto inventory check), and
+`last_scan_target`/`last_scan_candidate`. Counters are per USB connection, not
+per GUI session. A zero-candidate scan sends no pair command; the idle pause
+after it is waiting for an awake discoverable device, not a pairing retry.
+Manual slot operations reject while auto is enabled. Disable advances a
+generation and prevents later scan/pair dispatch, including after a scan already
+in flight; an already dispatched command can finish. Disconnect disables the
+policy, wakes pending notification waits and synchronously drains both delayed
+work items before freeing the endpoint. No work runs from the input IRQ path.
+See the [kernel workqueue API](https://docs.kernel.org/core-api/workqueue.html)
+for delayed-work cancellation and freezable queue semantics.
+
+Razer documents mouse/keyboard multi-device support on its
+[HyperFlux V2 product page](https://www.razer.com/eu-en/gaming-mouse-mats/razer-hyperflux-v2-wireless-charging-system).
+That establishes the product capability, not this reverse-engineered protocol's
+correctness on every firmware/model. Receiver discovery/inventory remains the
+authority for which compatible devices this implementation can target.
+
+### Membership versus sleep (release12)
+
+The read-only inventory observer now also publishes `hyperflux_mouse_pid` and
+emits a HID change event when mouse membership changes. Zero/unknown returns
+EAGAIN; only a successful inventory can publish a PID or empty `ffff`. Presence
+flags never remove a sleeping device. The daemon removes an unpaired mouse
+logical object and emits its normal device-removed signal, without rebinding
+or closing the pad's input interfaces. The keyboard's PID-specific group
+already follows the same membership rule. Failed/busy reads preserve entries.
+Both pads are isolated by physical HID path, not by a global model list.
+
+In release 12 the separate root `paired_device_pid` remained a probe-time mouse
+feature binding. That limitation is superseded by release 13 below.
+
+Polychromatic treats wireless ETIMEDOUT as connected/possibly sleeping and
+EBUSY as receiver discovery/pairing in progress. ENODEV is unavailable/stale,
+not proof of sleep. Technical traces are opt-in details; a retry only reloads
+controls, never replays a failed settings write. Unexpected errors remain
+distinguishable from these transient wireless states.
+
+### Dynamic mouse controls (release 13)
+
+The membership observer now creates `hyperflux_mouse_00a8` or
+`hyperflux_mouse_00e8` beneath interface four, including when the slot was empty
+at probe. Each directory contains the existing model-specific Naga controls and
+`paired_device_pid`. A successful inventory drives creation/removal and emits
+KOBJ_CHANGE; failed reads and sleep preserve the current child. Removal drains
+active sysfs callbacks before the model binding changes. PID-specific paths and
+live inventory checks prevent an old V2 child issuing commands to a new V3.
+Unrecognised mice retain input and pairing support without guessed controls.
+
+The daemon resolves the child path on initial enumeration and change events,
+retains its stable receiver/model identity, and emits normal device-added or
+device-removed notifications. Bounded read-only retries cover udev permission
+timing. Legacy root-level controls remain supported by the updated daemon.
+Registration does not restore settings, switch input mode, or require an awake
+wireless mouse. All USB HID interfaces remain bound and pass input unchanged.
+
+Offline checks exercise empty-slot boot, first pairing, V2/V3 replacement,
+unpair/re-pair, creation failure/retry, disappearing groups, legacy matching,
+and sleeping children. Hardware hotplug validation is still required after
+installing/loading this release; the live working receiver was not reloaded
+during development.
